1
00:00:00,433 --> 00:00:03,129
Tasks are what the name kind of implies.

2
00:00:03,803 --> 00:00:06,997
They're the tasks these agents should work

3
00:00:07,093 --> 00:00:07,222
on.

4
00:00:08,137 --> 00:00:10,400
So we have a research task, which will

5
00:00:10,608 --> 00:00:13,401
probably be fulfilled by the research agent

6
00:00:13,818 --> 00:00:16,001
and a reporting task, which will be handled

7
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:17,221
by the reporting agent.

8
00:00:18,168 --> 00:00:20,222
And the idea is that you could define

9
00:00:20,398 --> 00:00:22,629
multiple tasks and also define multiple

10
00:00:22,645 --> 00:00:25,197
tasks to a single agent. Here we have 2

11
00:00:25,229 --> 00:00:28,859
tasks for for 2 agents, so 1 task per agent

12
00:00:28,908 --> 00:00:29,100
though.

13
00:00:30,209 --> 00:00:34,032
Now, just like agents, tasks are configured

14
00:00:34,209 --> 00:00:36,763
with help of external configuration files.

15
00:00:37,598 --> 00:00:40,056
And that's what this tasks.yaml file is

16
00:00:40,249 --> 00:00:40,683
there for.

17
00:00:41,872 --> 00:00:44,233
In that file, we find 2 task definitions

18
00:00:44,265 --> 00:00:46,723
because we have 2 tasks. And every task has

19
00:00:46,771 --> 00:00:49,406
a description that describes what the task

20
00:00:49,406 --> 00:00:49,856
is about.

21
00:00:50,836 --> 00:00:53,181
And we also can have placeholders here,

22
00:00:53,310 --> 00:00:55,172
which makes sense because we might wanna

23
00:00:55,172 --> 00:00:57,532
perform the same kind of task on different

24
00:00:57,661 --> 00:00:59,218
inputs, on different topics.

25
00:01:00,487 --> 00:01:02,558
We have an expected output that should be

26
00:01:02,558 --> 00:01:05,882
produced by the agent for this task. And we

27
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:08,595
also assign which agent will perform this

28
00:01:08,595 --> 00:01:10,843
task, which of course is important to know.

29
00:01:11,662 --> 00:01:13,766
So you can think of these descriptions here

30
00:01:14,199 --> 00:01:17,025
basically as the user prompts that are sent

31
00:01:17,025 --> 00:01:18,454
to the large language model.

32
00:01:19,273 --> 00:01:21,359
The agent definitions here could be the

33
00:01:21,359 --> 00:01:24,039
system prompts, which in general control how

34
00:01:24,167 --> 00:01:25,387
the agent should behave.

35
00:01:26,012 --> 00:01:27,665
And then for the different tasks, we have

36
00:01:27,697 --> 00:01:30,474
the specific task descriptions that are sent

37
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:33,282
as user prompts to the large language model,

38
00:01:33,619 --> 00:01:34,036
kind of.

39
00:01:35,239 --> 00:01:37,775
So that's the task definitions file.

40
00:01:39,091 --> 00:01:41,337
Back in the crew.py file, we can also see

41
00:01:41,337 --> 00:01:43,568
that the second task has an output file

42
00:01:43,568 --> 00:01:46,039
configuration, which means that its output

43
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,057
will not be funneled into another agent, but

44
00:01:49,057 --> 00:01:50,373
instead into a file

45
00:01:51,047 --> 00:01:53,037
because we know that here for this program,

46
00:01:53,053 --> 00:01:55,204
for this crew, will be done once the

47
00:01:55,204 --> 00:01:56,793
reporting task is completed

48
00:01:57,531 --> 00:01:59,217
and the results should be stored in a

49
00:01:59,217 --> 00:02:00,485
reports.md file.

50
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:03,101
And of course, I know all that from the

51
00:02:03,101 --> 00:02:03,807
official docs.

52
00:02:04,353 --> 00:02:06,070
That's of course the place to dive into if

53
00:02:06,070 --> 00:02:07,435
you wanna learn more about CrewAI.

54
00:02:08,879 --> 00:02:11,961
Speaking of funneling results from one agent

55
00:02:11,993 --> 00:02:13,903
to another, that is what's happening behind

56
00:02:13,903 --> 00:02:16,292
the scenes here. So what we did manually in

57
00:02:16,469 --> 00:02:18,969
our code is done for us here by the CrewAI

58
00:02:18,969 --> 00:02:19,354
package.

59
00:02:20,155 --> 00:02:22,784
And it is done for us because of this last

60
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:25,541
thing here in the ResearchCrew class in the

61
00:02:25,541 --> 00:02:26,823
crew.py file.

62
00:02:27,705 --> 00:02:30,189
Here, the overall crew is defined and

63
00:02:30,189 --> 00:02:30,638
registered.

64
00:02:31,455 --> 00:02:34,260
And that happens by returning a crew object

65
00:02:34,276 --> 00:02:35,542
based on the Crew class.

66
00:02:36,264 --> 00:02:38,412
It's here where all these agents are then

67
00:02:38,524 --> 00:02:39,982
assigned to this crew

68
00:02:40,591 --> 00:02:42,306
and where the tasks are assigned.

69
00:02:43,012 --> 00:02:46,299
This process thing here controls that they

70
00:02:46,524 --> 00:02:48,047
are executed in order.

71
00:02:48,625 --> 00:02:51,078
And with that, the overall crew is

72
00:02:51,511 --> 00:02:54,623
registered and ready to go. That's the idea

73
00:02:54,623 --> 00:02:56,756
here. And behind the scenes, because all

74
00:02:56,884 --> 00:02:59,241
these agents and tasks belong to the same

75
00:02:59,354 --> 00:02:59,610
crew,

76
00:03:00,171 --> 00:03:02,545
the output from one task, which was handled

77
00:03:02,577 --> 00:03:06,041
by one agent, is provided to the next task

78
00:03:06,105 --> 00:03:08,382
in the next agent. That happens behind the

79
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:10,566
scenes automatically on our behalf.

80
00:03:12,044 --> 00:03:15,706
And that's all. But this is not too useful

81
00:03:15,819 --> 00:03:18,839
because none of these agents has any tools,

82
00:03:19,047 --> 00:03:21,489
so they won't really be able to do any

83
00:03:21,585 --> 00:03:22,854
valuable research.
